<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parakramabahu I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1123–<a href="/years/1186">1186</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Parakramabahu the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parakramabahu I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> from 1153 to 1186, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a>. After a long civil war he reunited the island, reformed and unified the Buddhist sangha, and built great irrigation works, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakrama-samudra">Parakrama Samudra</a>, and monuments such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gal-vihara">Gal Vihara</a>. He also sent military expeditions to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. He is regarded as one of the greatest Sinhalese kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-i.jpg" alt="Parakramabahu I" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Graham Racher from London, UK · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_of_Parakramabahu_in_Polonnaruwa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Parakramabahu is generally thought to have been born around 1123, almost certainly at Punkhagama, the capital of Dakkhinadesa, the southern of the island's three kingdoms. His father, Manabharana, ruled Dakkhinadesa and his mother was Ratnavali. Through his grandmother Mitta, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayabahu-i">Vijayabahu I</a>, he belonged to what the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Culavamsa</a> calls the Arya branch of the royal house, rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> branch that held Rajarata. The chronicle says a god-like figure foretold his birth in his father's dream.</p><p>As a young man he left Dakkhinadesa in secret for the court of Gajabahu II of Rajarata, having the general Sankha killed on the way when Sankha tried to report his journey. On his return he reluctantly met his uncle, Kitti Sri Megha, and on the uncle's death in 1140 he became king of Dakkhinadesa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In Dakkhinadesa he set up a capital of his own, Parakramapura, identified with modern Panduwas Nuwara, restored shrines and the Kotabaddha causeway over the Deduru Oya, and cut canals and cleared forest for new fields. Exports of pearls, gems, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> through Kalpitiya, Chilaw and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> supported him. Around 1150 he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> and defeated an army of Gajabahu; he directed the war against Rajarata without leading the invasion himself, basing his strategy on the writings of Kautilya.</p><p>From 1153 he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a>. His economic policy, also drawn from Kautilya, placed all trade, alcohol included, in the hands of the government. He set out to unite the three rival orders of the sangha and to correct monks who had married and pursued worldly gain, and he restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, including the Thuparamaya, Mihintale and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruwanwelisaya">Ruwanwelisaya</a>. Revolts in Ruhuna, joined in 1156 by Queen Sugala, mother of his old rival Manabharana, and a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in 1157 kept his generals Rakkha and Bhuta occupied.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a> in 1186 and was succeeded by Vijayabahu II, described as his sister's son, whom he had summoned from Sinhapura, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>. Historians such as Wilhelm Geiger note that he took no steps to secure a smooth succession; the instability and renewed civil war after his reign undid many of his works. His expeditions to the mainland cost him heavily without weakening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a>. Seven later kings took his name over the next four centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parakramabahu I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Parakramabahu I</b>, who ruled from 1153 to 1186, reunited the island after a civil war, built the vast reservoir called the Parakrama Samudra, reformed and unified the Buddhist sangha and sent expeditions to Burma and South India.</blockquote><a href="/state/polonnaruwa-kingdom">Polonnaruwa Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=7.93,81&amp;year=1163" aria-label="Polonnaruwa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Parakramabahu I"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4241 4324h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4241 4324h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=7.93,81&amp;year=1163">What was here in 1163? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
